'''''Lamium bifidum''''' is a species of [[flowering plant]] in the mint family [[Lamiaceae]], native to the [[Southern Europe]]. It was first described by [[botany|botanist]] [[Domenico Cirillo]] in 1788.

==Description==
''Lamium bifidum'' is  an [[annual plant|annual]] [[herbaceous plant]].<ref name=Osogovo /> It possesses an erect or decumbent stem, white flowers and a deeply bifid lower lip.{{sfn|Flamini|Cioni|Morelli|2005|p=63}}{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=92}} Flowers form in [[verticillaster]]s of 20 or more flowers.{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=92-93}} These flowers are sometimes [[cleistogamy|cleistogamous]].{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=10}}

An analysis of the [[essential oil]] of ''Lamium bifidum'' bracts and leaves identified the highest constituents as [[germacrene D]] (34.9%), [[β-caryophyllene]] (11.5%) and [[Humulene|α-Humulene]] (6.8%).{{sfn|Flamini|Cioni|Morelli|2005|p=66-67}} This contrasted with the primary constituents identified in the flowers: [[myrcene]] (47.2%), β-caryophyllene (11.8%) and [[sabinene]] (11.0%).{{sfn|Salehi|Armstrong|Rescigno|Yeskaliyeva|2019|p=4}}

The species possesses a chromosome count of 2n = 18.{{sfn|Olivencia|Téllez|1989|p=175}}{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=12}}

==Taxonomy==
Domenic Cirillo first described ''Lamium bifidum'' in his ''Plantarum Rariorum Regni Neapolitani'', published in 1788.
[[John Isaac Briquet]]'s 1897 taxonomy placed the species within the subsection ''Amplexicaulia'', within the larger section ''Pollichia''.{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=48}} [[Jacob Mennema]]'s 1989 revision of the genus narrowed its close relations, placing it instead within the Subgenus Lamium, in a smaller Section Lamium on the basis of morphological similarities, namely a straight [[corolla tube]] and presence of [[bracteoles]].{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=19}}{{sfn|Bendiksby|Brysting|Thorbek|Gussarova|2011|p=987}} This section consists of ''L. bifidum'' as well as ''[[Lamium purpureum]]'' and ''[[Lamium confertum]]''.{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=18}} 21st century [[phylogenetic]] studies brought this change into question however, finding a closer genetic relationship between ''L. bifidum'' and ''[[Lamium amplexicaule]]'' var. ''amplexicaule'' and ''[[Lamium incisum]]''.{{sfn|Bendiksby|Brysting|Thorbek|Gussarova|2011|p=992}}{{sfn|Krawczyk|Szczecińska|Sawicki|2014|p=280-281}} A 2016 study of [[pollen]] structuring provided further evidence for this closer relation to the species of section ''Amplexicaule'' rather than section ''Lamium''.{{sfn|Atalay|Celep|Bilgili|Doğan|2016|p=82}}

Authors have identified as many as four subspecies:
*subsp. ''bifidum''
*subsp. ''albimontanum'' Rech. f. - endemic to Crete{{sfn|Gómez Hernández|Pujadas salvá|2005|p=158}}
*subsp. ''balcanicum'' Velen. endemic to the Balkan Peninsula & Romania, specifically the west Moesian-south Dacian area<ref name=Osogovo />{{sfn|Veljić|Marin|Krivošej|Ljubić|2006|p=127}}
*subsp. ''gevorense'' Gómez Hern. - endemic to Spain - identified in 1977, but proposed in 2005 to be raised as a separate species, ''Lamium gevorense''{{sfn|Gómez Hernández|Pujadas salvá|2005|p=160}}
The ''albimontanum'' and ''balcanicum'' subspecies are distinguished from the type species by their pink or purple flowers.{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=94}}{{sfn|Gómez Hernández|Pujadas salvá|2005|p=158}}

''Lamium bifidum'' var. ''clandestinum'' was previously raised to the separate species of ''Lamium cryptanthum'', but this is now merely regarded as a synonym.{{sfn|Boullu|1879|p=165}}

==Distribution==
''Lamium bifidum'' has been described as having a broadly Mediterranean distribution.{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=18}} One or more subspecies have been documented as present in parts of Bulgaria, Corsica, Crete, Greece, Italy, Portugal, Romania, Sardinia, Spain, Tunisia and the former Yugoslavia.<ref name=UKWildflower /><ref name=EuroMed /><ref name=Osogovo /><ref name=POTW />

The species inhabits open spaces and clearings, waste places, mountain slopes and forest edges.<ref name=Osogovo />{{sfn|Mennema|1989|p=94}}

==Uses==
A chemical constituent of ''Lamium bifidum'', [[hesperetin]], has identified anti-estrogen, tumorigenesis and breast cancer cell inhibition properties.{{sfn|Koirala|Thuan|Ghimire|Thang|2016|p=105}}

